export const ABSTRACT = [
  'Purana Qila (Old Fort), on Mathura Road in New Delhi, is a sixteenth-century fortified complex associated with Humayun’s city of Din Panah and the subsequent rule of Sher Shah Suri. Excavations beneath the visible monument indicate occupation across multiple historical periods. For this report, we studied government announcements, heritage descriptions, archaeological publications, and a University of Delhi teaching text. We also included observations from our group’s site visit and analysed the survey responses collected from 20 visitors. It examines political chronology and architecture; compares reported excavation sequences and artefacts; evaluates the proposed association with ancient Indraprastha; and audits recurring public claims for evidentiary strength.',
  'Our research shows that the site has evidence of occupation during several historical periods. However, evidence of early settlement does not by itself prove the ancient name of the place or confirm a story described in a literary text. Sources differ in their dating and description of Painted Grey Ware (PGW), and a complete excavation record was not readily available in the sources reviewed. The report also analyses the group’s completed visitor survey (n = 20), using the original respondent-level CSV records. Results indicate that most respondents recognise the involvement of both Humayun and Sher Shah, accept a multi-period archaeological sequence, and choose a cautious or uncertain position on the Indraprastha connection. Historical-explanation clarity ratings are mixed. Throughout the report, we separate information from written sources, survey responses, what our visiting members observed, and questions that remain unresolved.',
];
